{"id":"monazite","name":"Monazite","formula":"(Ce,La,Nd,Th)PO₄","chronometers":"U–Th–Pb (EPMA, LA-ICP-MS, SIMS), (U–Th)/He","system":"Monoclinic","point_group":"2/m","setting":"P2₁/n","lattice":{"a":6.7902,"b":7.0203,"c":6.4674,"alpha":90,"beta":103.38,"gamma":90},"lattice_source":"Handbook of Mineralogy; Ni et al. (1995)","elongation_axis":[0,1,0],"mono_color":"#c98a3e","view":{"tilt":18,"turn":-40},"forms":[{"key":"100","label":"{100}","index":[1,0,0],"name":"a, tabular face","color":"#c0762e"},{"key":"101","label":"{101}","index":[1,0,1],"name":"w","color":"#e2b15b"},{"key":"-101","label":"{1̅01}","index":[-1,0,1],"name":"x","color":"#d79a5a"},{"key":"110","label":"{110}","index":[1,1,0],"name":"m, termination","color":"#7d5a8c"},{"key":"011","label":"{011}","index":[0,1,1],"name":"e, termination","color":"#5a7d8c"}],"matrix":{"title":"Tabular on {100} × elongation along b","subtitle":"Rows flatten the crystal on a{100}, its commonest habit. Columns elongate it along [010], with m{110} and e{011} terminations.","row_title":"a{100} development","col_title":"Elongation along b","rows":[{"label":"A1","detail":"equant zone"},{"label":"A2","detail":"a{100} moderate"},{"label":"A3","detail":"tabular on a"},{"label":"A4","detail":"strongly tabular"}],"cols":[{"label":"E1","detail":"equant"},{"label":"E2","detail":"1.6:1"},{"label":"E3","detail":"2.5:1"},{"label":"E4","detail":"4:1"}],"note":"No habit typology exists for monazite, so this grid spans the habits Dana (1892) and the Handbook of Mineralogy describe. The signs of the w/x {±101} and r/v {±111} pairs are uncertain in the old literature, so both members of {±101} are used at the same distance and {±111} is left out. Cruciform twins on {100} are not modelled."},"about":"\n<p>Monazite is the main REE-phosphate petrochronometer. Its common habit is tabular on a{100}. Crystals can also be\nelongated along b, in the zone of a, c and the {±101} forms, and terminated by m{110} and e{011}. Some are prismatic on\nv{111}, equant, or wedge-shaped. Old monoclinic morphology uses the acute β, so the h index changes sign when\nconverting to the modern P2₁/n cell. Metamorphic monazite is often anhedral, and growth kinetics control its shape\n(Ayers et al. 1999).</p>","habits":[{"name":"Tabular on a{100}","text":"The commonest habit (rows A3–A4)."},{"name":"Elongate along b","text":"The zone a + {±101}, terminated by m{110} and e{011} (columns E3–E4)."},{"name":"Equant / wedge-shaped","text":"Short crystals in all directions (cell A1·E1)."},{"name":"Not modelled","text":"Prismatic habits on v{111}, whose sign convention is uncertain, and cruciform twins on {100}."}],"references":[{"citation":"Engi, M. (2017). Petrochronology based on REE-minerals: monazite, allanite, xenotime, apatite. <i>Reviews in Mineralogy and Geochemistry</i> 83, 365–418.","doi":"10.2138/rmg.2017.83.12","url":null,"note":"Review of monazite and xenotime petrochronology.","tags":["review"]},{"citation":"Williams, M.L., Jercinovic, M.J., Hetherington, C.J. (2007). Microprobe monazite geochronology: understanding geologic processes by integrating composition and chronology. <i>Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences</i> 35, 137–175.","doi":"10.1146/annurev.earth.35.031306.140228","url":null,"note":"In-situ monazite dating tied to textures and growth domains.","tags":["review"]},{"citation":"Ayers, J.C., Miller, C., Gorisch, B., Milleman, J. (1999). Textural development of monazite during high-grade metamorphism: hydrothermal growth kinetics, with implications for U,Th-Pb geochronology. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 84, 1766–1780.","doi":"10.2138/am-1999-11-1206","url":null,"note":"Links monazite crystal shape and size to growth kinetics.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Rasmussen, B., Muhling, J.R. (2007). Monazite begets monazite: evidence for dissolution of detrital monazite and reprecipitation of syntectonic monazite during low-grade regional metamorphism. <i>Contributions to Mineralogy and Petrology</i> 154, 675–689.","doi":"10.1007/s00410-007-0216-6","url":null,"note":"Habit and shape of low-grade syntectonic monazite.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Overstreet, W.C. (1967). The geologic occurrence of monazite. <i>U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper</i> 530.","doi":"10.3133/pp530","url":null,"note":"A classic compilation of monazite occurrence and habit.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Boyce, J.W., Hodges, K.V., Olszewski, W.J., Jercinovic, M.J. (2005). He diffusion in monazite: implications for (U-Th)/He thermochronometry. <i>Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems</i> 6, Q12004.","doi":"10.1029/2005GC001058","url":null,"note":"He diffusion, where grain geometry acts as the diffusion domain.","tags":["thermochronology"]},{"citation":"Ni, Y., Hughes, J.M., Mariano, A.N. (1995). Crystal chemistry of the monazite and xenotime structures. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 80, 21–26.","doi":"10.2138/am-1995-1-203","url":null,"note":"Structure of monazite and xenotime.","tags":["lattice"]}],"cells":[{"id":"A1E1","row":0,"col":0,"label":"A1·E1","description":"equant zone (a{100} at 1× the {±101} distance); equant along b.","default":false,"params":{"face_distances":{"100":1.0,"101":1.0,"-101":1.0,"110":1.1,"011":1.1},"cap_translation":0.0},"fractions":{"100":0.0998,"101":0.1398,"-101":0.1877,"110":0.2704,"011":0.3023},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surface_area":5.32308,"rsv":0.56358,"sphericity":0.90849,"aspect":1.1631},"geometry":{"vertices":[[-0.53305,-0.28915,0.26842],[-0.53305,-0.28915,-0.19092],[0.53305,-0.28915,-0.26842],[-0.08993,-0.06275,-0.73016],[-0.41801,-0.41141,-0.33092],[0.0,-0.85564,-0.02111],[0.32808,-0.50698,-0.42034],[0.53305,0.28915,-0.26842],[0.53305,-0.28915,0.19092],[0.53305,0.28915,0.19092],[-0.53305,0.28915,-0.19092],[-0.53305,0.28915,0.26842],[0.08993,-0.06275,0.73016],[0.41801,-0.41141,0.33092],[-0.32808,-0.50698,0.42034],[0.0,-0.85564,0.02111],[-0.32808,0.50698,0.42034],[-0.0,0.85564,0.02111],[0.08993,0.06275,0.73016],[0.41801,0.41141,0.33092]